ct liability proponents want to tie up future medical discovery models in pre-market clearance delays that stretch past 36 months. The Department of Health and Human Services estimates that chronic diseases cost the United States economy 3.8 trillion dollars annually, a burden that autonomous biomedical accelerators could slash in half if permitted to deploy at software speed. When regulators choose precautionary paralysis, they actively choose to let patients die of diseases we have the compute to cure today. We reject that fatal trade.\n\nApplying negligence standards instead of strict liability protects the capability frontier through clear enforcement mechanisms overseen by the Federal Trade Commission under Section 5 unfairness authority. A coalition of startup founders, open-source researchers, and patient advocacy groups supports this framework, opposing legacy tech lobbyists who use strict liability to crush competitive pressure. Historical precedent from the 1996 Telecommunications Act proves that immunity from unworkable carrier liability sparked the internet economy, and foundation models require the exact same legal oxygen to cure cancer and automate labor drudgery. Congress and the judiciary must reject strict liability before regulatory paralysis locks in permanent technological stagnation.",
